🔹 Step 1: Running the Experiment

Simply run the full script I provided. It will:
	1.	Randomly initialize the transformer (no pre-training needed).
	2.	Run a simple training loop (only 100 epochs, very lightweight).
	3.	Track embeddings, attention, and feedforward singular values over time.
	4.	Save visualizations in the transformer_experiment/ directory.

✅ What you’ll get after running it:
	•	loss_curve.png → Cross-entropy loss over epochs.
	•	embeddings_epoch_0.png → PCA of embeddings before learning.
	•	embeddings_epoch_50.png → PCA of embeddings midway.
	•	embeddings_epoch_100.png → PCA of embeddings after learning.
	•	umap_embeddings_epoch_0.png → UMAP version of embeddings before training.
	•	attention_epoch_0.png → Self-attention matrix at epoch 0.
	•	attention_epoch_50.png → Self-attention midway.
	•	attention_epoch_100.png → Self-attention after learning.
	•	ffn_singular_values.png → How the rank of feedforward outputs changes




🔹 Step 2: Analyzing the Results

Once the experiment completes, check the saved images.
	1.	Look at the PCA & UMAP embeddings (embeddings_epoch_*.png)
	•	Do the points cluster more as training progresses?
	•	If they do, this suggests structured learning.
	•	If they remain random, it might indicate not enough training or a missing pattern.
	2.	Examine self-attention heatmaps (attention_epoch_*.png)
	•	If attention maps start random but later focus on specific words, this suggests that attention follows a self-organizing pattern.
	•	If attention remains random, try increasing the number of epochs.
	3.	Check the Singular Value Collapse (ffn_singular_values.png)
	•	If singular values decrease over training, the model is compressing information, meaning the FFN is reducing dimensionality efficiently.